  "worked_solution": "Given a normal random variable $Y∼N(\\mu ,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge y)=P(Z\\ge \\frac{y-\\mu }{\\sigma })\n$$\n\nwhere $Z∼N(0,1)$ is a standard normal random variable. In our case, we have $\\mu =0$ and $\\sigma =5.$\n\nFirst, we transform $Y$ into a standard normal random variable $Z$ by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y\\ge 4.1) & =P(Z\\ge \\frac{4.1-0}{5}) \\\\  & =P\\,(Z\\ge \\frac{4.1}{5}) \\\\  & =P(Z\\ge 0.82)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/differential/14263116/assets/e52cf18a535093c62068e0de8b4319dce833a5637b80ffb15a4c738efbdeebce.png)\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 0.82) & =1-P(Z<0.82) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 0.82) \\\\  & =1-Φ(0.82)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, we know that\n\n$$\nΦ(0.82)=0.7939.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Z\\ge 0.82) & =1-0.7939 \\\\  & =0.2061.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(Y\\ge 4.1)=0.2061.\n$$",
